          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "**If you are having thoughts of harming yourself or ending your life, get help now.** In the US, call or text 988. Elsewhere, call your local emergency number or a crisis line. If you are in immediate danger, call emergency services. Tell someone nearby how you are feeling, and stay with them if you can. If you are not having these thoughts, please tell me so, and I'll take that into account.\n\nChest pressure brought on by stress or exertion, or shortness of breath, needs prompt medical assessment, not waiting.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed.** A questionnaire score is not a diagnosis. It prompts a further assessment, which includes ruling out physical causes such as thyroid disease and heart rhythm problems. A clinician also reviews substance, caffeine and medication effects, and checks for depression, which often occurs alongside anxiety. The guidance excerpt I could read stops before the questionnaire score ranges, so I can't give you those thresholds. Generalized anxiety disorder means persistent, hard-to-control worry about many everyday things, most days, for at least six months. Only a clinician can decide whether that applies to you.\n\n**What is first-line.** The guidance describes two first-line options of broadly comparable effectiveness, chosen together with the person:\n- Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), given one-to-one, in a group, or through a guided digital program. It has the largest evidence base of the talking therapies.\n- Medication from the selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor or serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor classes.\n\nI found no reported disagreement between authorities on these options. I can't give doses or say which suits you.\n\n**Is insomnia treated separately?** The insomnia guidance recommends CBT for insomnia as first-line, ahead of medication. Its effects last after treatment ends, and it avoids the risks of long-term sleeping tablets. It also lists anxiety among the conditions to screen for when someone presents with insomnia. I could not find whether insomnia alongside anxiety should be treated as one problem or two, so I can't answer that part.\n\n**What to do next.** Book an appointment with your doctor soon. Tell them about the worry, the sleep problems, how long they have lasted, and any thoughts of self-harm. Ask whether a check for physical causes has been done, and whether CBT-based approaches for anxiety and for insomnia are available to you. If those thoughts are present or getting stronger, don't wait for the appointment. Use the crisis line above.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
